{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/personalisation--education",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "personalisation (education)"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The orientation of learning provision towards the individual person — that is, attending to each learner's prerequisites, interests, pace, and meaning-making."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "As a sub-concept of difference-sensitive pedagogy, personalisation is analytically distinct from individualisation (organisational internal differentiation) and differentiation (methodological variation), even though the terms frequently blur in practice. Personalisation addresses difference at the level of the single person, not of the group or of societal categories. Difference is regarded as individual singularity: each person develops a distinct perspective through their circumstances (cf. the assumption of uniqueness alongside equal rights). Difference is here construed as personal rather than social-categorical. The critical blind spot of the concept is, that a purely personalised perspective can render structural inequality invisible by translating social categories into individual attributes."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "5.2.5"
  ],
